    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d314">

                <pb facs="0401" n="299" xml:id="pg299"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3020</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sammons</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>)<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d314-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-02-21"><hi rendition="#italic">February 21,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2687</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to record that the
                    Internal Peace Conference of China opened here, on yesterday, in the former
                    German Club, the delegations being as follows— <list>
                        <item><hi rendition="#italic">For the North:</hi> Chu Chi-chien, Wu
                            Ting-chang, Wang Ko-ming, Sze Yu, Fan Chu, Wang Yu-ling, Liu Un-kou, Li
                            Kuo-tseng, Kiang Shao-chi, and Hsu Fu-su.</item>
                        <item><hi rendition="#italic">For the South:</hi> Tong Shao-yi, Tsang
                            Chi-chao, Hu Han-ming Mao Chia-so, Tseng Yen, Kuo Tsung-sung, Liu
                            Kuang-lieh, Wang Pah-chung, Peng Yung-yi, Yao Ming-luan, and Li
                            Shi-yin.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>The initial session opened at 9:00 o’clock in the morning, and it is proposed to
                    adhere to early morning activities during future sessions.</p>
                <p>Further, as a matter of record, there is enclosed herewith the speeches made by
                    the chief delegates for the North and for the South, and other data relative to
                    the conference, the same having appeared in today’s issue of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Shanghai Times</hi><anchor n="20" xml:id="d314-f2"/>
                    (accepted as being pro-Japanese in its policies). This newspaper’s account of
                    the opening session is more connected and more convenient for filing, as a
                    matter of record, than the accounts appearing in the other newspapers.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thomas Sammons</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
